#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119556 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95f2e191-0760-5cb8-b55a-cd2955150e82" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equation of an Osculating Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "b1d808a6-49e1-54ae-88c7-36c42f93e179" :question/math-academy-id "e-3060" :question/problem "![](images/19/1990d7cb1625ca97f10debf834883f6e434473f154cf9683db60ee7c4a69c83e.png)\n\nFind a parametric equation of the osculating plane for the curve  $\\mathbf r(t)$ at the point where $t=1,$ given that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle -2t , \\: t^2 , \\: \\dfrac{1}{3}t^3 \\right\\rangle, \\qquad\n\\mathbf T(1) = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\left\\langle -2 , 2 , 1 \\right\\rangle, \\qquad\n\\mathbf N(1) = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\left\\langle 2 , 1 , 2\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The osculating plane passes through the point $P,$ which is at the tip of the vector $\\mathbf r(1).$ The vectors $\\mathbf T(1)$ and $\\mathbf N(1)$ are parallel to this plane and are not collinear, so a parametric equation for the plane can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(s,u) = \\mathbf r(1) + s \\, \\mathbf T(1) + u \\, \\mathbf N(1), \\qquad s,u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing $\\mathbf r(1),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨-2(1),\\,(1)^{2},\\,\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3}⟩=⟨-2,1,\\frac{1}{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a parametric equation for the osculating plane at the point $\\left(-2,1,\\dfrac 1 3 \\right)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s,u) & =r(1)+s\\,T(1)+u\\,N(1) \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,\\frac{1}{3}⟩+s\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,2,1⟩+u\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨2,1,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-6-2s+2u,\\,3+2s+u,\\,1+s+2u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119557 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119741 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68feb21c-0d21-5889-8d1e-2eb4b27382af" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of an Osculating Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "0ecbb0ee-5559-55c3-9aba-0056288a6c67" :question/math-academy-id "e-3061" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the osculating plane for the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ at the point where $t=0,$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k \\\\ T(t) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k) \\\\ N(t) & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡tk).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the Cartesian equation of the osculating plane, we need to compute the position vector $\\mathbf p$ and a vector $\\mathbf n$ that is perpendicular to the osculating plane. \n\nThe position vector of the point where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p} = \\mathbf{r}(0) = \\cos{0}\\,\\mathbf i+\\cos{0}\\,\\mathbf j+\\sqrt{2}\\sin{0}\\, \\mathbf k = \\langle 1, 1, 0 \\rangle.\n$$\n\nTo compute the perpendicular vector, we first need to compute the unit tangent $\\mathbf T(0)$ and the principal normal $\\mathbf N(0)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\,j+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\,k)=k \\\\ N(0) & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\,j+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0k)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the cross product of these two vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =k\\times (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(-i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are interested only in the direction of the vector above, we can multiply it by a constant to yield a simpler vector that points in the same direction:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n} = \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt 2}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\left(\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j\\right) =\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nFinally, let $\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ denote the position vector of a general point in the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $\\mathbf{n}$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $\\mathbf{p},$ its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-1,0⟩ & =⟨1,1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-1,0⟩ \\\\ 1\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot y+0\\cdot z & =0 \\\\ x-y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/ad/ad22c676123844c2428febb34e0218cfcb05917154e8cff994f35fa4fbf68944.png)\n\n**Note:** Because our curve is a plane curve, it has the same osculating plane at each point. As shown above, the osculating plane coincides with the plane of the curve. \n\nThis result holds in general: any plane curve has the same osculating plane at each of its points (wherever the osculating plane is defined)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119558 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119742 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a445fa-d44b-574a-8369-b30cac3a3c96" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Shortest Distance Between an Osculating Plane and the Origin"}] :question/id #uuid "7b4c22d8-65f0-5a5e-9eb5-754f778a7bf7" :question/math-academy-id "e-3062" :question/problem "![](images/46/46e9f26846b088dfe225b36b889c788ed847c08376295f02dc2c9fbaf72a1645.png)\n\nFind the shortest distance between the origin and the osculating plane for the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡6t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡6t,\\,8t⟩ \\\\ T(t) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡6t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡6t,\\,4⟩ \\\\ N(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡6t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡6t,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the Cartesian equation of the osculating plane, we need to compute the position vector $\\mathbf p$ and a vector $\\mathbf n$ that is perpendicular to the osculating plane. \n\nThe position vector of the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p} = \\mathbf{r}\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\left\\langle \\sin{\\left(6\\cdot\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)} , \\:  -\\cos{\\left(6\\cdot\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)}, \\: 8\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\right\\rangle  = \\left\\langle -1, 0, 2\\pi \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTo compute the perpendicular vector, we need to compute the unit tangent $\\mathbf T \\left( \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\right)$ and the principal normal $\\mathbf N \\left( \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\right) \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,4⟩=\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,-3,4⟩ \\\\ N(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,0⟩=⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the cross product of these two vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =T(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times N(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,-3,4⟩\\times ⟨1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4\\,j+3\\,k) \\\\  & =⟨0,\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are interested only in the direction of the vector above, we can multiply it by a constant to yield a simpler vector that points in the same direction:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n} = 5\\cdot \\left\\langle 0 , \\dfrac{4}{5} , \\dfrac{3}{5} \\right\\rangle = \\langle 0, 4, 3  \\rangle\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{|⟨-1,0,2\\pi ⟩\\cdot ⟨0,4,3⟩|}{‖⟨0,4,3⟩‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pi }{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486460 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95f2e191-0760-5cb8-b55a-cd2955150e82" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equation of an Osculating Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "037fb055-c287-54d8-9dc3-90ff9d19082b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨0,\\,1+u,\\,s-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485771} {:answer/id #uuid "1ab9bb6c-62ce-5b10-8119-470f2ae83911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨0,\\,u+s,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485813} {:answer/id #uuid "56d6bcbd-a2c8-56ee-892f-4ed3a57c0242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨0,\\,2-u,\\,s⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485919} {:answer/id #uuid "94609f09-9021-5e3b-ab30-6bb7a78b6ebf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨u,\\,2+s,\\,3u⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486032} {:answer/id #uuid "eefd776e-2271-55c8-9e20-cf811c0469c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨1,\\,s-u,\\,2u⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56d6bcbd-a2c8-56ee-892f-4ed3a57c0242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨0,\\,2-u,\\,s⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485919} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5c09547-1e15-5c60-92ba-f36758f345fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f746d7b9-67e2-5aa7-b32a-b632bdb15929" :question/math-academy-id "q-127137" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the osculating plane for the curve  $r(t)$ at the point where $t=0,$ given that\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{3},\\,2-{t}^{2},\\,t⟩,\\,T(0)=⟨0,0,1⟩,\\,N(0)=⟨0,-1,0⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The osculating plane passes through the point $P$, which is at the tip of the vector $r(0).$ The vectors $T(0)$ and $N(0)$ are parallel to this plane. Therefore, a parametric equation for the plane can be written as\n\n$$\nR(s,u)=r(0)+s\\,T(0)+u\\,N(0),\\,s,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨{0}^{3},\\,2-{0}^{2},\\,0⟩=⟨0,2,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, a parametric equation for the osculating plane at the point $(0,2,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s,u) & =r(0)+s\\,T(0)+u\\,N(0) \\\\  & =⟨0,2,0⟩+s⟨0,0,1⟩+u⟨0,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2-u,\\,s⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95f2e191-0760-5cb8-b55a-cd2955150e82" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equation of an Osculating Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38c1e0ab-2828-5e9c-9df8-5e2c181ea0fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,(s-u)\\sqrt{3},\\,(s+u)\\sqrt{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375827} {:answer/id #uuid "9e76d983-055b-554b-bc9b-36fe5b1ed5a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨-\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,1-\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,1+\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375828} {:answer/id #uuid "c3bd6cff-b279-5c03-a2c3-437153fecca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨-\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3}-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375829} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e799c3-9581-586b-8d38-bfd28a7079b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨1+\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,1-\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375830} {:answer/id #uuid "267277fb-aa68-5bfb-abab-4bd34d2544b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,(s+u)\\sqrt{3},\\,(s-u)\\sqrt{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e76d983-055b-554b-bc9b-36fe5b1ed5a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨-\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,1-\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,1+\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375828} :answer-field/id #uuid "187786e6-deb0-5d64-8f2a-517c86ffee30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375826}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f4a870e-ec48-5855-9ff2-1d02c3e15a46" :question/math-academy-id "q-127278" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the osculating plane for the curve  $r(t)$ at the point where $t=0,$ given that\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,T(0)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}⟨-1,-1,1⟩,\\,N(0)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨0,-1,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The osculating plane passes through the point $P$, which is at the tip of the vector $r(0).$ The vectors $T(0)$ and $N(0)$ are parallel to this plane. Therefore, a parametric equation for the plane can be written as\n\n$$\nR(s,u)=r(0)+s\\,T(0)+u\\,N(0),\\,s,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡0⟩=⟨0,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, a parametric equation for the osculating plane at the point $(0,1,1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s,u) & =r(0)+s\\,T(0)+u\\,N(0) \\\\  & =⟨0,1,1⟩+s\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}⟨-1,-1,1⟩+u\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨0,-1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,1-\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,1+\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375853 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119741 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68feb21c-0d21-5889-8d1e-2eb4b27382af" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of an Osculating Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cce7a57-ec50-5b7d-a10d-d12d340f7ebd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375855} {:answer/id #uuid "0dd603f8-dae9-5232-88ef-942f6c34a5d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375856} {:answer/id #uuid "e673e372-7f75-50af-8159-f2472454cfed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+z-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375857} {:answer/id #uuid "98881dce-2ee9-5276-ac33-8fcb4aca837c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375858} {:answer/id #uuid "4a92762f-efaa-559b-abe8-23eb75919596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-z=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cce7a57-ec50-5b7d-a10d-d12d340f7ebd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375855} :answer-field/id #uuid "38507475-0514-5b9b-b6cc-a25300e0334e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5bbe6880-9115-5b6f-a9bb-cfd1553b146d" :question/math-academy-id "q-127295" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the osculating plane for a curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(0,1,2)$ where $t=\\pi ,$ given that\n\n$$\nT(\\pi )=i,\\,N(\\pi )=-k.\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the position vector of the point $P(0,1,2)$ is $p=⟨0,1,2⟩.$\n\nTo find the equation of the osculating plane, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to it. To do that, we find the cross product of the unit tangent $T(1)$ and the principal normal $N(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1)\\times N(1) & =i\\times (-k) \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our perpendicular vector $n$ is \n\n$$\nn=T(\\pi )\\times N(\\pi )=j=⟨0,1,0⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ denote the position vector of a general point on the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $n$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $p$, its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,1,0⟩ & =⟨0,1,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\ x(0)+y(1)+z(0) & =0(0)+1(1)+2(0) \\\\ 0+y+0 & =0+1+0 \\\\ y-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375860 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119741 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68feb21c-0d21-5889-8d1e-2eb4b27382af" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of an Osculating Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cecf739-72ba-5487-96f6-0db04f45b2ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375862} {:answer/id #uuid "a5405c60-125e-50c7-b4d5-cad7b7311891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y-z+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375863} {:answer/id #uuid "077d07a8-a85d-5d26-b5b2-3cf85ef93746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y+z-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375864} {:answer/id #uuid "e123dac7-b1cf-5d1d-a2fe-d98f864d5b00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375865} {:answer/id #uuid "5defad4f-2e3b-53f8-83af-3aa31470ddde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cecf739-72ba-5487-96f6-0db04f45b2ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375862} :answer-field/id #uuid "91d9bf77-7863-573d-8373-7f2526605345" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "569b65f9-8938-5c3e-b130-07826f3d58f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-127301" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t=0,$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2,\\,-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ N(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the unit tangent and the principal normal at the point where $t=0$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0⟩=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\ N(0) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡0⟩=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the position vector of the point where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\np=r(0)=⟨{e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,2,\\,-{e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0⟩=⟨0,2,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(0)$ and the principal normal $N(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\times N(0) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,-1⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our perpendicular vector $n$ is \n\n$$\nn=T(0)\\times N(0)=⟨0,-1,0⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ denote the position vector of a point on the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $n$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $p$, its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,-1,0⟩ & =⟨0,2,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,-1,0⟩ \\\\ x(0)+y(-1)+z(0) & =0(0)+2(-1)+(-1)(0) \\\\ 0-y+0 & =0-2+0 \\\\ y-2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486418 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119742 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a445fa-d44b-574a-8369-b30cac3a3c96" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Shortest Distance Between an Osculating Plane and the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2874ff9e-7c47-56db-be1b-11c5fdf0e83b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{42}}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485832} {:answer/id #uuid "3397ab0b-df84-52a4-8928-ed9be664a840" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485860} {:answer/id #uuid "385126ea-dc1b-5c5d-b072-8b099e1dd3fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485873} {:answer/id #uuid "7eaa1135-2317-5f3a-b304-5805e9937caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{42}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485989} {:answer/id #uuid "e71fd7c6-683f-52a6-a59b-6ea4e8a3f5d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7eaa1135-2317-5f3a-b304-5805e9937caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{42}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485989} :answer-field/id #uuid "82d8b5c1-785d-5e26-8c7b-3a060c72900c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "982718cb-04c5-5f70-b128-45fa2db5cdc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-127314" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the origin and the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(1,-1,5)$ where $t=0,$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}⟨-2,-1,3⟩,\\,N(0)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the position vector of the point $P(1,-1,5)$ is $p=i-j+5\\,k.$\n\nWe need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(0)$ and the principal normal $N(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =T(0)\\times N(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}⟨-2,-1,3⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}[\\left|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}(-4\\,i+5\\,j-\\,k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|(i-j+5\\,k)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}(-4\\,i+5\\,j-\\,k)| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}|-4-5-5| \\\\  & =\\frac{|-14|}{\\sqrt{42}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{42}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486438 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119742 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a445fa-d44b-574a-8369-b30cac3a3c96" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Shortest Distance Between an Osculating Plane and the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0021ee10-91bc-58f5-b793-de1ecf1c0e80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485761} {:answer/id #uuid "30125279-c825-536a-a28b-b52b09ab4d97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485857} {:answer/id #uuid "3d25a864-e849-588a-920a-51554e070b68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485883} {:answer/id #uuid "48a7f27f-c839-5095-afb1-5803d86ab7fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485897} {:answer/id #uuid "5684cc7d-d22a-5c88-80f2-971688a492a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0021ee10-91bc-58f5-b793-de1ecf1c0e80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485761} :answer-field/id #uuid "8915730a-0d54-5b73-a211-633a544404bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c6f5e861-f6b7-50af-904e-74f27b7e3031" :question/math-academy-id "q-127317" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the origin and the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t=\\pi ,$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ T(t) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,4,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ N(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the unit tangent and the principal normal at the point where $t=\\pi $ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,4,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩=\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,-3⟩ \\\\ N(\\pi ) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩=⟨-1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the position vector of the point where $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\np=r(\\pi )=⟨-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,4\\pi ,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩=⟨3,4\\pi ,0⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(\\pi )$ and the principal normal $N(\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =T(\\pi )\\times N(\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,-3⟩\\times ⟨-1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\,j+4\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|⟨3,4\\pi ,0⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,4⟩| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}|0+12\\pi +0| \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120270 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119741 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68feb21c-0d21-5889-8d1e-2eb4b27382af" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of an Osculating Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69b74c9f-1ac7-5a46-a1ba-f698684da9b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+\\sqrt{3}z+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485942} {:answer/id #uuid "69efef97-f312-5209-9f6a-670aa33a1ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}x-z-\\sqrt{3}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485943} {:answer/id #uuid "ee6844a6-528c-589f-8c8f-0e27539f7d38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-z+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486197} {:answer/id #uuid "f230ed97-e97b-5244-a435-92e949f80f8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-z+\\sqrt{3}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486204} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b4d79d-0b34-5b15-9b62-727ead6d0a6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+\\sqrt{3}z-\\sqrt{3}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69efef97-f312-5209-9f6a-670aa33a1ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}x-z-\\sqrt{3}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485943} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1e11bc9-cec7-5abb-9e74-1d0988c65cb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3005bcca-c33e-585c-8e84-018875446bff" :question/math-academy-id "q-33665" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t=0,$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{t}^{2}\\,k \\\\ T(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\sqrt{3}\\,k) \\\\ N(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the unit tangent and the principal normal at the point where $t=0$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\,j+\\sqrt{3}\\,k)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,k \\\\ N(0) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\,j=j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the position vector of the point where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\np=r(0)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)\\,i+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0)\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}(0)^{2}\\,k=⟨1,0,0⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(0)$ and the principal normal $N(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\times N(0) & =(\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,k)\\times j \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are interested only in the direction of the above vector, let's write our perpendicular vector $n$ as \n\n$$\nn=2\\cdot (T(0)\\times N(0))=-\\sqrt{3}\\,i+k=⟨-\\sqrt{3},0,1⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ denote the position vector of a point on the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $n$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $p$, its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\sqrt{3},0,1⟩ & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\sqrt{3},0,1⟩ \\\\ -\\sqrt{3}\\cdot x+0\\cdot y+1\\cdot z & =-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{3}x-z-\\sqrt{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486451 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119741 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68feb21c-0d21-5889-8d1e-2eb4b27382af" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of an Osculating Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "176152ad-a4fc-5fee-b7a4-595a11f250cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y+2z=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485805} {:answer/id #uuid "8728317b-82f4-56f1-85cd-753c47282979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-2z=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486000} {:answer/id #uuid "8e943a0a-8be6-5cea-b466-bb3405a21996" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-2z=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486019} {:answer/id #uuid "a65161fd-61e5-5848-af7f-d066aafc5c45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-2z=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486065} {:answer/id #uuid "e262438a-c34f-5e29-a898-9a26026a1d36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y-2z=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8728317b-82f4-56f1-85cd-753c47282979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-2z=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486000} :answer-field/id #uuid "83bce5eb-d032-50f1-80c7-b8e437dbc204" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0dccf1d-9f50-573c-8356-a6a604a3b7cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-33691" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the osculating plane for a curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(0,1,1)$ where $t=0$, given that\n\n$$\nT(0)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(\\,i+\\,j+\\,k),\\,N(0)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\,i-\\,j).\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the position vector of the point $P(0,1,1)$ is $p=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nTo find the equation of the osculating plane, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to it. To do that, we find the cross product of the unit tangent $T(0)$ and the principal normal $N(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\times N(0) & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(\\,i+\\,j+\\,k))\\times (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\,i-\\,j)) \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}(i+j-2\\,k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are interested only in the direction of the above vector, let's write our perpendicular vector $n$ as \n\n$$\nn=\\sqrt{6}\\cdot (T(0)\\times N(0))=i+j-2\\,k=⟨1,1,-2⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ denote the position vector of a general point on the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $n$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $p$, its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,-2⟩ & =⟨0,1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,-2⟩ \\\\ 1\\cdot x+1\\cdot y+(-2)\\cdot z & =-1 \\\\ x+y-2z & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120276 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119741 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68feb21c-0d21-5889-8d1e-2eb4b27382af" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of an Osculating Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11282053-dd73-5914-8543-21a4d764485f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485794} {:answer/id #uuid "2637249f-ab89-5b78-a794-b710b767ee33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485829} {:answer/id #uuid "603393fc-bd9e-57f9-b876-32bff3244313" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485931} {:answer/id #uuid "d0cdb536-9cf5-51b0-8412-cf8b20ca8551" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x-y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486140} {:answer/id #uuid "fbb0c11a-bfed-5de2-90cf-418349bcc1f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "603393fc-bd9e-57f9-b876-32bff3244313" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485931} :answer-field/id #uuid "b16a9277-3747-5cfc-a2ea-e503ba88803b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "857f2717-334c-550e-bce1-cd3a3ad47f82" :question/math-academy-id "q-33782" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the osculating plane for a curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(1,0,0)$ where $t=1$, given that\n\n$$\nT(1)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\,i+\\,j),\\,N(1)=k.\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the position vector of the point $P(1,0,0)$ is $p=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\nTo find the equation of the osculating plane, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to it. To do that, we find the cross product of the unit tangent $T(1)$ and the principal normal $N(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1)\\times N(1) & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\,i+\\,j))\\times k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are interested only in the direction of the above vector, let's write our perpendicular vector $n$ as \n\n$$\nn=\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (T(1)\\times N(1))=i-j=⟨1,-1,0⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ denote the position vector of a general point on the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $n$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $p$, its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-1,0⟩ & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-1,0⟩ \\\\ 1\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot y+0\\cdot z & =1 \\\\ x-y-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95f2e191-0760-5cb8-b55a-cd2955150e82" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equation of an Osculating Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01fac468-a7b6-5712-8e0b-41e91e5a6051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\frac{1}{2}⟨2\\pi +\\sqrt{2}s,\\,-2+2u,\\,-\\sqrt{2}s⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485768} {:answer/id #uuid "4601c7f6-a843-5519-a8d0-884713da0c54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\frac{1}{2}⟨-\\sqrt{2}s,\\,2\\pi ,\\,-2+2u⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485895} {:answer/id #uuid "954e222a-6dbe-56d9-b273-83af84e2ff36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,\\,-\\sqrt{2}s,\\,-\\pi +u⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486033} {:answer/id #uuid "bd2d4665-ae08-5b34-a2ff-c288d6296066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨-2,\\,2\\pi +\\sqrt{2}s,\\,-\\sqrt{2}s⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486102} {:answer/id #uuid "cac7cce1-50a0-5479-9788-34f8656af02e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨-2+2u,\\,-\\sqrt{2}s,\\,\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01fac468-a7b6-5712-8e0b-41e91e5a6051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\frac{1}{2}⟨2\\pi +\\sqrt{2}s,\\,-2+2u,\\,-\\sqrt{2}s⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485768} :answer-field/id #uuid "37f3d36c-d151-51a5-9ff3-aa273e1e2fed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "091c6a72-5e82-5c94-a2c2-b3675bcf57e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-34082" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the osculating plane for the curve  $r(t)$ at the point where $t=\\pi $, given that\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,T(\\pi )=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨1,0,-1⟩,\\,N(\\pi )=⟨0,1,0⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The osculating plane passes through the point $P$, which is at the tip of the vector $r(\\pi ).$ The vectors $T(\\pi )$ and $N(\\pi )$ are parallel to this plane. Therefore, a parametric equation for the plane can be written as\n\n$$\nR(s,u)=r(\\pi )+s\\,T(\\pi )+u\\,N(\\pi ),\\,s,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩=⟨\\pi ,-1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, a parametric equation for the osculating plane at the point $(\\pi ,-1,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s,u) & =r(\\pi )+s\\,T(\\pi )+u\\,N(\\pi ) \\\\  & =⟨\\pi ,-1,0⟩+s\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨1,0,-1⟩+u\\cdot ⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨2\\pi +\\sqrt{2}s,\\,-2+2u,\\,-\\sqrt{2}s⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393744 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95f2e191-0760-5cb8-b55a-cd2955150e82" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equation of an Osculating Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ec9aa97-f0f7-5a10-8249-361b901e466a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}},\\,\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393746} {:answer/id #uuid "de4add72-0cd4-537b-80b7-0002434bbdf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨1+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,1-\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}},\\,1-\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393747} {:answer/id #uuid "250743fd-4960-51b1-885b-3ed93deac3de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨1+3s,\\,2s+3u,\\,2s-3u⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393748} {:answer/id #uuid "07171637-65de-5c83-8235-9dc05a85624e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨1+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}},\\,\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,1+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393749} {:answer/id #uuid "28f201ce-0d59-5c71-83a0-18e65a037a4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨2+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}},\\,1-\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,3+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07171637-65de-5c83-8235-9dc05a85624e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨1+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}},\\,\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,1+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393749} :answer-field/id #uuid "78aa28f8-2396-55a6-bbc4-e9955564a261" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf74894d-e339-586f-8261-369741108e3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-77651" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the osculating plane for the curve  $r(t)$ at the point where $t=0$, given that\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,T(0)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,\\,1,\\,1⟩,\\,N(0)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The osculating plane passes through the point that is at the tip of the vector $r(0).$ The unit tangent vector $T(0)$ and the principal normal vector $N(0)$ are parallel to this plane. Therefore, a parametric equation for the plane can be written as \n\n$$\nR(s,u)=r(0)+sT(0)+uN(0),\\,s,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨{e}^{0},\\,{e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,{e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0⟩=⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, a parametric equation for the osculating plane at the point $(1,\\,0,\\,1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s,u) & =r(0)+sT(0)+uN(0) \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩+s\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,\\,1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}},\\,\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,1+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486361 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95f2e191-0760-5cb8-b55a-cd2955150e82" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equation of an Osculating Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07a7aa8e-8250-5e7f-b1da-fd6ba9c04a5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=s\\,i+s\\,j+u\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485782} {:answer/id #uuid "12ba1c73-772e-515c-aec8-34b305694147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=(1+s)\\,i+(1+u)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485798} {:answer/id #uuid "d90c6099-11c2-5d7d-a5e5-729c0b284b71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=s\\,i+(s+u)\\,j+u\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486157} {:answer/id #uuid "dafcde51-97da-58e7-9199-f816378440a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=s\\,i+u\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486162} {:answer/id #uuid "e41f7d8f-7802-5a7b-ac95-2b235a0f1a14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=(1+s)\\,i+(1+u)\\,j+u\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dafcde51-97da-58e7-9199-f816378440a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=s\\,i+u\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486162} :answer-field/id #uuid "098c49d2-d4b0-51d4-b086-0a8b1106923a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1cbb1ea4-6349-500a-851f-5c768482f036" :question/math-academy-id "q-78207" :question/problem "Find the equation of the osculating plane (in parametric form) for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t=0$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+{t}^{3}\\,k,\\,T(0)=i,\\,N(0)=j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The osculating plane passes through the point that is at the tip of the vector $r(0).$ The vectors $T(0)$ and $N(0)$ are parallel to this plane. Therefore, the equation of the plane (in parametric form) can be written as\n\n$$\nR(s,u)=r(0)+s\\,T(0)+u\\,N(0),\\,s,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =0\\,i+{0}^{2}\\,j+{0}^{3}\\,k=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the equation of the osculating plane at the point $(0,0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s,u) & =r(0)+s\\,T(0)+u\\,N(0) \\\\  & =0+s(i)+u(j) \\\\  & =s\\,i+u\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486424 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119742 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a445fa-d44b-574a-8369-b30cac3a3c96" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Shortest Distance Between an Osculating Plane and the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04c1b22e-b816-57ee-bbe8-37a69e36b8f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485776} {:answer/id #uuid "2e052709-5e44-5985-99e4-06b1462a0108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485850} {:answer/id #uuid "332c74c7-bdf2-5231-82e7-a24032edbbdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485859} {:answer/id #uuid "6f3ce111-408b-5e8c-9cbd-cdf8b9b63b76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485955} {:answer/id #uuid "d278362e-19ce-5e02-a5a6-6a1e323289bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e052709-5e44-5985-99e4-06b1462a0108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485850} :answer-field/id #uuid "7486bba8-f0d3-5fb8-8123-8cbb33a7b160" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ad7e0597-ae11-56bc-bce9-c805c73a6765" :question/math-academy-id "q-79231" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the origin and the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(4,0,4)$ where $t=-2$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-2) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,\\,\\sqrt{2},\\,1⟩ \\\\ N(-2) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the position vector of the point $P(4,0,4)$ is $p=⟨4,\\,0,\\,4⟩.$\n\nWe need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(-2)$ and the principal normal $N(-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =T(-2)\\times N(-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,\\,\\sqrt{2},\\,1⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & \\sqrt{2} & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}(\\sqrt{2}\\,i+2\\,j-\\sqrt{2}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨1,\\sqrt{2},-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|⟨4,0,4⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}⟨1,\\sqrt{2},-1⟩| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}|4+0-4| \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486378 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119742 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a445fa-d44b-574a-8369-b30cac3a3c96" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Shortest Distance Between an Osculating Plane and the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f44f82f-0911-50e6-9955-8927e6dd0cff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485928} {:answer/id #uuid "90f4e46f-b8dc-57a7-9776-c1970edf16db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486026} {:answer/id #uuid "ca2315d4-5189-559b-9833-b72d4cf16c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486129} {:answer/id #uuid "e84a642c-b9c7-5a08-aa9a-27173d4089c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486191} {:answer/id #uuid "f76e5d23-1c27-5b09-a2a5-f67217c56999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90f4e46f-b8dc-57a7-9776-c1970edf16db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486026} :answer-field/id #uuid "57b2e81e-78c5-5bf6-853c-77f6b9242995" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "47ce2283-c10d-5cc5-8c7c-2556a8407b56" :question/math-academy-id "q-79233" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the origin and the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t=-\\pi ,$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨{sin}^{3}⁡t,{cos}^{3}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ T(-\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,-4⟩ \\\\ N(-\\pi ) & =⟨-1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that  the position vector of the point where $t=-\\pi $ is\n\n$$\np=r(-\\pi )=⟨{sin}^{3}⁡(-\\pi ),{cos}^{3}⁡(-\\pi ),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2\\pi )⟩=⟨0,-1,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(-\\pi )$ and the principal normal $N(-\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-\\pi )\\times N(-\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,-4⟩\\times ⟨-1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|⟨0,-1,1⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,3⟩| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}|\\,0-4+3\\,| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486416 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119742 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a445fa-d44b-574a-8369-b30cac3a3c96" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Shortest Distance Between an Osculating Plane and the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1da6f0c3-76e7-5503-844e-8622cb924f20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485815} {:answer/id #uuid "739256f1-b769-53b4-ab47-ce2e94f23fb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485964} {:answer/id #uuid "9abf1575-0060-5c79-9872-176be0c29808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486042} {:answer/id #uuid "a84d026a-b54d-52db-a1d5-b85362a18293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486070} {:answer/id #uuid "cf15716a-e90d-5a02-b950-3aebc77a484a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf15716a-e90d-5a02-b950-3aebc77a484a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486137} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c400157-4106-5d0d-874d-0beda0bc2e09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486252}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "955e768a-bc00-543e-ac79-17f131f88a54" :question/math-academy-id "q-79234" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the origin and the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t=0$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =({t}^{2}+\\sqrt{2})\\,i+(\\sqrt{2}-{t}^{2})\\,j+2t\\,k \\\\ T(0) & =k \\\\ N(0) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i-\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the position vector of the point where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\np=r(0)=(0+\\sqrt{2})\\,i+(\\sqrt{2}-0)\\,j+0\\,k=⟨\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2},0⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(0)$ and the principal normal $N(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =T(0)\\times N(0) \\\\  & =k\\times (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i-\\,j)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|⟨\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2},0⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,1,0⟩| \\\\  & =|1+1| \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
